{"name":"Frosty Snow Cap","hex":"#accde5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frosty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Cold, chilly; icy.","Having frost on it or in it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frosty"},{"term":"snow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The partly frozen, crystalline state of water that falls from the atmosphere as precipitation in flakes; also, the falling of such flakes; and the accumulation of them on the ground or on objects as a white layer.\n\n An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm.","An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Preceded by the dummy subject it: to have snow (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1) fall from the atmosphere.","Of a thing: to fall like snow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snow"},{"term":"cap","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Chipaya."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A close-fitting hat, either brimless or peaked.","A special hat to indicate rank, occupation, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cap"}],"entries":[{"title":"Snow","blocks":[{"text":"Snow is a form of solid atmospheric precipitation consisting of individual ice crystals that grow while suspended in the air—usually within clouds—and then fall, accumulating on the ground, where they undergo further changes. It consists of frozen crystalline water throughout its life cycle, starting when, under suitable conditions, the ice crystals form in the atmosphere, increase to millimeter size, precipitate and accumulate on surfaces, then metamorphose in place, and ultimately melt, slide, or sublimate away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg/330px-CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow","wikidata":"Q7561"},{"title":"Cap","blocks":[{"text":"A cap is a flat headgear. They made their first appearance as early as 3200 BC. The origin of the word \"cap\" comes from the Old French word \"chapeau\" which means \"head covering\". Over time, the word has evolved and changed its meaning, but it still retains its association with headwear. They are popular in casual and informal settings, and are seen in sports and fashion. They are typically designed for warmth, and often incorporate a visor to block sunlight from the eyes. They come in many shapes and sizes and are of different brands. Baseball caps are one of the most common types of cap."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cap","wikidata":"Q6147804"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Feb242007_blizzard.gif/500px-Feb242007_blizzard.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Feb242007_blizzard.gif/1280px-Feb242007_blizzard.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"ht